from .common import pick, truth, written

NOTES = {
    1: ['Transmission media come in two families. **Guided** (wired) media keep the signal inside a physical path: **twisted pair**, **coaxial cable** and **fibre-optic cable**. **Unguided** (wireless) media send the signal through the air: **radio waves**, **microwaves** and **infrared**.',
        'Exam questions on this deck are mostly “which medium fits this situation?” scenarios. For each medium, know what it is made of, one or two advantages, one or two disadvantages, and a typical use; for the wireless ones, also know the frequency range, whether it is omnidirectional or line-of-sight, and whether it passes through walls.'],
    2: ['The **transmission medium** (or channel) is the **physical path** between the transmitter and the receiver: a cable or the air. It sits **below** the physical layer (some books call it layer 0) and is **controlled by** the physical layer, which decides what signals to put on it. So the medium itself isn’t a layer; it is what the bottom layer uses.'],
    3: ['**Guided** media guide the signal along a physical conductor, so it goes only where the cable goes: **twisted pair** and **coaxial** (both copper, carrying electrical signals) and **fibre-optic** (glass or plastic, carrying light).',
        '**Unguided** media have no physical conductor: the signal spreads through the air (or space) and any device with a suitable receiver can pick it up. The three types are **radio waves**, **microwaves** and **infrared**, listed from lowest to highest frequency.'],
    4: ['The three guided media, from cheapest and slowest to most expensive and fastest: **twisted pair** (used in nearly every office LAN), **coaxial cable** (cable TV, old Ethernet), and **fibre-optic** (backbones, long distances, high speed).'],
    5: ['A typical Ethernet cable holds **four twisted pairs** inside one plastic jacket, each pair with its own colour code (orange, green, blue, brown, each paired with a white-striped wire). The colours let installers wire both ends the same way.'],
    6: ['A **twisted pair** is two **insulated copper wires** twisted around each other in a regular spiral. The two wires together form **one communication link**: one carries the signal and the other is the reference, and the receiver looks at the **difference** between them.',
        'Why twist them? To **reduce electrical interference** (noise and crosstalk) from other pairs close by and from outside sources. Twisting makes both wires pick up the same interference, which then cancels out at the receiver (slide 8 shows how). The tighter the twisting, the better the cancellation, so **more twists per length means better quality**.',
        'Twisted pair is used in the **telephone network** between a house and the local exchange (the subscriber loop) and in **LANs**, where it is the standard Ethernet cable.'],
    7: ['The figure shows the difference twist length makes. **Category 3** UTP has long twists, every 7.5 to 10 cm. **Category 5** UTP is twisted much more tightly, every 0.6 to 0.85 cm. Because Cat 5 cancels interference better, it can carry **higher frequencies** and so **higher data rates** with less crosstalk. This is the practical meaning of “more twists = better quality”.'],
    8: ['(a) With **two parallel wires**, one wire is always closer to the noise source, so it picks up more noise than the other. The receiver reads the **difference** between the wires, and an unequal amount of noise leaves a leftover (+6 in the figure) that corrupts the signal. (b) With a **twisted pair**, the two wires keep swapping positions along the cable, so over its length **both pick up the same total noise**. When the receiver subtracts one from the other, the noise **cancels** (a difference of 0), and only the real signal remains.'],
    9: ['**UTP** is Unshielded Twisted Pair: the pairs sit directly inside a plastic cover. **STP** is Shielded Twisted Pair: the pairs are wrapped in a **metal shield** (foil or braided mesh) inside the plastic cover. The shield blocks outside electromagnetic interference before it reaches the wires.'],
    10: ['**STP**: the metal braid or covering **reduces interference**, so it suits electrically noisy places such as factories. The price: it is **more expensive** and **harder to handle**, because it is thicker and heavier and the shield must be grounded properly.',
         '**UTP**: the **cheapest** cable and the **easiest to install**, which is why almost every office LAN uses it. The trade-off: it **suffers from external electromagnetic interference (EMI)**. So the choice depends on the environment: an ordinary office uses UTP, a site full of motors or heavy equipment uses STP (or fibre).'],
    11: ['The categories are standards for how good a twisted-pair cable is. A **higher category** supports a **higher bandwidth** (in MHz, how high a frequency it can carry) and so a **higher data rate**: CAT5e 100 MHz / 1 Gbps, CAT6 250 MHz / 1 Gbps, CAT6a 500 MHz / **10 Gbps**, CAT7 600 MHz / 10 Gbps, CAT8 2000 MHz / 25 or 40 Gbps. CAT5e to CAT6a can be UTP or STP.',
         'From **CAT7 upward the cable must be shielded**. At such high frequencies, interference and crosstalk are too strong for unshielded pairs. Even then, the top speed only works over short runs: shielded CAT8 reaches 40 Gbps only up to about 24 m, which is why it is used inside data centres.'],
    12: ['**RJ** stands for **Registered Jack**. The **RJ-45** is the 8-pin connector used on twisted-pair Ethernet cables. The female socket is in the NIC, switch or wall plate; the male plug is crimped onto the cable. (A phone cable uses the smaller RJ-11.)'],
    13: ['In 10 and 100 Mbps Ethernet, only four of the eight pins carry data. **Pins 1 and 2** transmit: **TD+** and **TD-** (Transmit Data positive and negative). **Pins 3 and 6** receive: **RD+** and **RD-**. Each signal uses a pair (+ and -) because the receiver reads the difference between the two wires, as in slide 8.',
         '**Pins 4, 5, 7 and 8** are **N/C**, meaning **No Connection** or not connected: unused in 10/100 Ethernet. Gigabit Ethernet uses all eight pins, sending on all four pairs at once.'],
    14: ['A **crossover cable** connects one end’s **transmit** pins (1, 2) to the other end’s **receive** pins (3, 6), and the other way round: 1 to 3, 2 to 6, 3 to 1, 6 to 2. That way, what one device sends lands on the other device’s receive pins.'],
    15: ['**Straight-through** cables wire each pin to the same pin at the other end (1 to 1, 2 to 2, 3 to 3, 6 to 6). They connect **different kinds of device**: a **PC to a switch or hub**, or a **router to a switch or hub**. This works because a switch already swaps transmit and receive internally, so the PC’s transmit pins meet the switch’s receive pins.',
         '**Crossover** cables connect **the same kind of device**: **PC to PC**, **hub to hub**, **switch to switch**, **hub to switch**. Two identical devices both transmit on pins 1 and 2, so a straight cable would connect transmit to transmit and nothing would be received; the crossover swaps the pairs. Memory rule: unlike devices use straight, like devices use cross. (Many modern ports detect and swap automatically, a feature called auto-MDIX, but exams expect the rule.)'],
    16: ['**Coaxial cable** has a central **copper wire** (the inner conductor) inside a layer of **insulation**, surrounded by an **outer conductor** of copper mesh (braid or foil), and an **outer plastic jacket**. The outer conductor completes the circuit and shields the inner wire from interference, so coax carries higher frequencies than twisted pair. It is used for **cable TV** and was used in the old **bus Ethernet** (10Base5 thick coax and 10Base2 thin coax).'],
    17: ['An optical fibre has three layers from the inside out. The **core** is one or more very thin strands of glass or plastic, and it is what carries the light.',
         'Around the core is the **cladding**, glass or plastic with **different optical properties** from the core (a lower refractive index). That difference makes light that hits the boundary bounce back into the core instead of escaping. The outer **jacket** is plastic that protects the fibre from moisture, crushing and other damage.'],
    18: ['The figure shows how light travels along a fibre: it **reflects** off the boundary between the core and the cladding again and again, zig-zagging down the core from the sender to the receiver. Because the light stays inside, very little signal is lost and nothing leaks out to be picked up by others.'],
    19: ['Fibre’s advantages: **higher bandwidth** (data rates of hundreds of Gbps); **smaller size and lighter weight** than copper; **lower attenuation**, so repeaters can be much farther apart, **at least 50 km** compared with about 100 m for UTP; **no crosstalk**, since no light leaks from one fibre to another; it is **not affected by electromagnetic noise**, because light isn’t disturbed by electrical fields; and it is **highly secure**, because no light leaks out for someone to tap.',
         'Its disadvantages: installing it **needs special skills** (fibres have to be cut, polished and joined precisely), and it is **more expensive** (the cable, connectors and equipment). A common exam trap lists “cheap” or “easy to install” as advantages of fibre; they are its disadvantages.'],
    20: ['The table compares the two main LAN cables. **UTP**: 10 Mb/s to 10 Gb/s, **relatively short distances (up to 100 m)**, **low immunity** to EMI, radio interference and electrical hazards, but the **lowest cost** and the **lowest installation skill**.',
         '**Fibre**: 10 Mb/s to 100 Gb/s, **long distances (up to 100,000 m, i.e. 100 km)**, **completely immune** to EMI, radio interference and electrical hazards (glass doesn’t conduct electricity), but the **highest cost** and the **highest installation skill**. Use the table as a decision tool: long distance, electrical noise or a high-voltage environment points to fibre; a short, ordinary office run points to UTP.'],
    21: ['The three unguided (wireless) media, from **lowest to highest frequency**: **radio waves**, **microwaves**, **infrared**. As the frequency goes up, signals become more directional and worse at going through walls.'],
    22: ['With unguided media the signal travels through the air, so it is **available to everyone** who has a device that can receive it. That has two consequences: wireless is convenient (no cables, mobile devices), but anyone nearby can listen in, so **security** (encryption) matters much more than on a cable.'],
    23: ['The electromagnetic spectrum, **frequency increasing from left to right**: **radio waves and microwaves** from 3 kHz to 300 GHz (radio below 300 MHz, microwaves from 300 MHz to 300 GHz), then **infrared** from 300 GHz to 400 THz, then **visible light** from about 400 to 900 THz. A question that asks “which has the highest frequency?” among radio, microwave and infrared has infrared as the answer.'],
    24: ['**Radio waves** are below **300 MHz**. They are **omnidirectional**: the antenna sends the signal out **in all directions**, so the sending and receiving antennas don’t need to be lined up. They can **travel long distances** and pass through walls. That makes them suited to **broadcasting**: AM and FM radio and television, where one transmitter reaches everyone in the area. The downside is that anyone can receive them and nearby transmitters on the same frequency interfere.'],
    25: ['Radio waves are used for **multicast** communication: **one sender, many receivers**, as in radio and television broadcasting. Contrast this with microwaves (unicast) on slide 27. If a question describes one transmitter reaching many listeners, the answer is radio waves.'],
    26: ['**Microwaves** run from **300 MHz to 300 GHz**, the upper part of the radio range.',
         'Microwaves are **unidirectional**: they travel in a straight line, so the antennas must **see each other**, which is called **line-of-sight** propagation. That makes them good for **point-to-point** links, for example between two buildings to connect their LANs, with a dish on each roof pointing at the other. Because they are high frequency, they have a short wavelength and **have trouble passing through walls**: higher frequency means shorter wavelength, which means more difficulty getting through obstacles.',
         'Microwaves are used in **wireless networks**, **satellite** links and **cellular phones**: in all of them, microwaves carry the information through the air.'],
    27: ['Microwaves are used for **unicast** communication: one sender to **one** specific receiver, as in **cellular telephones**, **satellite networks** and **wireless LANs**. The directional beam is what makes one-to-one links practical. Remember the pair: radio = multicast, microwave = unicast.'],
    28: ['**Infrared** runs from **300 GHz to 400 THz**, just below visible light. It has a **very large bandwidth**, but it **cannot penetrate walls**, so it only works for **short-distance, local** communication, within one room. It also needs **line of sight** (directional): the remote has to point at the TV.',
         'It is used for **local point-to-point** links: **remote controls**, some wireless headphones and some wireless computer peripherals. Devices use the **IrDA** (Infrared Data Association) protocol.',
         'It **cannot be used under the sun**, because sunlight contains a lot of infrared that interferes with the signal. For most device-to-device links it has been **replaced by Bluetooth**, which uses radio and doesn’t need line of sight.'],
    29: ['Infrared’s one-line summary: **short-range** communication in a **closed area**, using **line-of-sight** propagation. The fact that it can’t leave the room is also an advantage: an infrared signal in one room doesn’t interfere with one in the next room, and it can’t be overheard from outside.'],
}

